														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1503525">
									

<p>It's an improvement, but I still generally don't approve of transmissions which cannot be blocked. And even if you removed the transmission aspect of it, it'd then just be an object which makes the nearest 20,736 humans 'go nuts for no apparent reason' (if you replace &quot;nuts&quot; with &quot;increased suggestibility, severe abulia, and short-term memory loss&quot;).</p>
<p>It also strikes me as an object where the Foundation is being held hostage by a K-class scenario if we don't do what the object &quot;wants&quot; (not that I'm implying the object is sapient), where what it &quot;wants&quot; would constitute 'excessive resources' (if nuclear weapons and blind guards are excessive resources, then requiring the political stagnation of a nation-state most certainly is). If the Foundation couldn't contain the effects better than this, and believed the item would bring about a K-class scenario if we didn't <em>provide it with twenty-one thousand human subjects</em>, then I don't see the Foundation not doing everything in its considerable power to neutralize and destroy this item.</p>
<p>This is even in spite of the Foundation's general policy to not destroy things - I'd point out the general definition of &quot;Keter&quot; (from the widely accepted <a href="/keter">Keter guide page</a> includes &quot;Such subjects must be cataloged, contained according to special containment procedures, <span style="text-decoration: underline;">and destroyed, if possible</span>&#8230; <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Research into the neutralization of Keter-class SCPs is always a top priority for the Foundation</span>&#8230; <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Cases where Keter-class objects that can be neutralized by Foundation personnel persist in Foundation custody are rare</span>&quot; (emphasis added). I don't see this item being both Keter class (a rating I agree with, given the stated effects) and having a compelling reason for it not being destroyed.</p>

							</div>

								
					
