														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1503547">
									

<p>Now, maybe your headcanon has a nuke-free Foundation, but the Foundation is generally considered a nuclear-armed power - from the <a href="/secure-facilities-locations">Secure Facilities Locations</a> Guide:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Area:</strong> Facility built to contain Keter-class objects. Constructed far from civilian establishments, Areas house nuclear devices that can be used to clear the facility and terminate all facility personnel. These devices are detonated if the containment of a Keter-class object is breached and is not able to be contained again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Soviets had lots of reliability issues with their nukes (the Western powers had issues too, just less frequently), and as the Foundation is fictional and under Rule of Drama things tend to not work properly in fictional settings, but I don't see the Foundation not destroying this. Hell, if you want, have North Korea destroy it with their 2006 nuclear test. The estimated blast yield of that was one kiloton, which is to say about 1/15th of the weapons used by the US in WWII, and 1/50th the size of &quot;small&quot; tactical nukes in the American and Soviet arsenals. But even so, it's still more than enough to both vaporize and disintegrate (in the strictest definition of both those terms) pretty much anything sitting immediately next to the bomb. End result (assuming only 1&#160;kt yield): tiny bits of bronze shrapnel scattered over an area the better part of a kilometer in radius, and a neutralized threat. And, if you scale up to a larger boom than the DPRK's atomic pop-gun, then you're talking about even smaller pieces spread over an even larger area, and an even more neutralized threat.</p>
<p>Also, the original <em>Homo erectus</em> origin isn't even hinted at in the article - or if it is, it's so subtle that I didn't catch it.</p>

							</div>

								
