			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1504285">
									

<p>If the Foundation knew who made this (or even had speculation), that would be in the file - especially if it was an explanation for why they knew it was base-twelve, and/or an explanation for why the Soviets couldn't destroy it and the Foundation inexplicably hadn't even bothered to try. The fact that there is absolutely zero mention about why the Foundation hasn't even tried to nuke the damn thing (682 has an explanation for that at least - nuking it probably would make matters worse) when it would be substantially less resource-intensive exercise than guaranteeing that some back-water authoritarian regime stayed in power is completely nonsensical to me. Based solely off the information in the article itself, there is no reason to believe that the damned thing is indestructible - and if it is (and it's just not mentioned in the article itself), there's no good reason for the thing to actually <em>be</em> indestructible beyond the out-of-universe desire to keep the in-universe Foundation (or GOC) from blowing it to hell and gone. And, of all the reasons to have something be indestructible, that's one of the worse ones.</p>
<p>I guess, in my opinion at least, while your comments about Easter eggs in this discussion thread are intriguing (and I use Easter eggs in essentially all of my own articles and tales myself), I'd have to say that the lack of discussion of these aspects of the object <span style="text-decoration: underline;">within the article itself</span> leaves them as plot holes, rather than Easter eggs.</p>

